 "observations": [
  "The page number «166» stands at the head of the page, centred, with a rule below it; at the left the number of the table, «Tab. 55», in bold sans serif, underlined. The page has no signature. The titles (L01-L04), in bold, the sigla in roman: N's title, O's «Elotari», C's «Examinatio Mercurii»; L04, «Locus1) draconis eius 0. 21. 10.», in smaller bold type, the figures set close, followed by «(CON)». The folios of the manuscripts stand at the left of L03 and L04, «C 70r, O 127v,» and «N 35v.», the letters raised; here as left margin notes. Specks at the top right and in the left margin; not transcribed.",
  "The table: rules and heads as on p. 165 (JP2 0197), with «Augmentum proprietatum omnium2)» and «Diminutio proprietatum omnium3)», «Sublimatio ascensus4) Mercurii examinata5)», «Examinatio ipsius elheza6)», «Examinatio centri», «Statio prima Mercurii» and «Latitude Mercurii descendentis7)» over «prima» and «secunda». Here each head is set from the top, one printed line to a line (L05-L09), the cells separated by our «|»; a head followed by empty cells spans their columns. L10, the units, in bold.",
  "L11-L40. Checked as on p. 162 (JP2 0194): the Sublimatio is 7s 14° 54′ less half the Examinatio argumenti in every row, a half minute counted as a whole, except at 150° (L40): there 7s 7° 21′ is 1′ more than the 7s 7° 20′ that the Examinatio argumenti of the row, 15° 8′, requires. No note of the apparatus bears on the 21; notes b concern the degrees.",
  "L11-L40. The Examinatio argumenti answers to an epicycle radius between 21.99 and 22.10 in every row; against an epicycle of radius 22.0 it is within 0.9′ from 121° to 134° and 1.5′ to 5.8′ larger from 135° on (150°: 15° 8′ for 15° 2.2′). The Latitudo secunda is 6° 15′ · sin x to within 0.5″ in every row except at 142° (L32), and its value for 180° − y is that of y on p. 163 (JP2 0195) except there: at 142° the edition prints O's 3° 50′ 51″ (note d), where C's 52 agrees with the computation, 3° 50′ 52.4″, and with the 3° 50′ 52″ of 38°.",
  "L11-L40, the other columns: the Examinatio centri decreases from 3° 24′ at 121° by 2′ to 4′ a degree, to 1° 57′ at 150°. To 136° it lies between 0.4′ below and 2.1′ above 3° 56′ · sin x, and its values are those of 180° − x on p. 163 (JP2 0195) or 1′ to 2′ smaller; from 136° to 137° it falls by 6′, 2° 45′ to 2° 39′, and from 137° on it is 1′ to 3.7′ below 3° 56′ · sin x and 2′ to 4′ smaller than the values of 180° − x (2° 39′ at 137° against 2° 42′ at 43°; 1° 57′ at 150° against 1° 59′ at 30°). No note of the apparatus bears on the column. The Statio prima is 4s 24° 29′, its least value, to 128° (from 115° on, p. 165, JP2 0197) and then increases by 0′ or 1′ a degree to 4s 24° 36′ at 149° and 150°. The Latitudo prima decreases from 1° 6′ at 121° and 122° by 1′ every second degree, every third at 133°-135°, 142°-144° and 147°-149°, to 0° 53′ at 150°.",
  "Notes on the readings. Note a: the last argument, 5s 0°, is N's; C and O have 4. Checked: the degrees 7 of the Sublimatio at 149° and 150° (notes b, O and N, against C's 6) agree with the rule; the Latitudo secunda 4° 6′ 1″ at 139° (note c, O and N, against C's 50), 4° 6′ 1.3″ computed. Notes 2-3: O and C's heads «computationis et numeri et diminutio (augmentum) examinis», as on p. 148 (JP2 0180, notes 2-3). The last note reports words that O adds in the upper margin, an Arabic sentence in Latin letters, with the editor's question marks.",
  "N03-N04: «La-titudo», divided at the end of the line with a hyphen; N04, the ellipsis of the lemma has three points, «. . .», where the lemmata of the other pages have four. Italics: «om.», «addid. in marg. superiore:». L04, «0. 21. 10.»; L05, «Latitude»; N02, the lemma «proprietatum omnium]» without a siglum; N03, «CO) —» and «om. C. —»; N06, «flscerafehu»: see the critical notes."
 ],
